  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Locally heavy rainfall will remain a threat for \nportions of southeast Georgia and southeast South Carolina into \nthis evening. \n\nThe first time period of interest is through the early morning \nhours as upstream convection across the SC Midlands progresses \nsouthward into portions of southeast SC and southeast GA. \nCurrent radar imagery shows a large cluster of convection across\nOrangeburg, Bamberg, and Barnwell counties that continues to \ntrack to the south and will reach Allendale, Colleton, and \nDorchester counties soon. Current thinking is that the \nCharleston Tri-County region should be pretty worked over from \nearlier convection and that the downstream focus will be across \nthe SC counties from Colleton County to the Savannah River, and \nalso southeast GA including Savannah. This convection has \ndisplayed a desire to propagate with a bit of a western \ncomponent with time as new updrafts initiate along outflow. The \nmain issues with this convection will be wind gusts into the 30-\n40 mph range as well as a period of heavy rainfall. These \nshowers and storms are moving at about 20 kt, so the heavy \nrainfall shouldn't last long enough to yield a significant flood\nthreat. This convection should reach the coast in approximately\n3 hours and then shift out over the coastal waters. \n\nFocus then turns to the daytime hours. Aloft, a well defined \ntrough will drop southward from the Upper Midwest and central \nAppalachians into VA and the Carolinas late in the day. This \nwill push a surface cold front southward as well. The front is \ncurrently analyzed from west to east across NC, on the cusp of \npushing into northern SC. The front is progged to make steady \nsouthward progress and will likely be on the northern edge of \nthe forecast area around or just after sunrise. By the \nafternoon, the front will likely be near the Savannah River and \nwon't clear the Altamaha River until the late evening hours. \nRegarding convective chances, for southeast SC the best chances \nwill come in the morning before the front clears the area and \nnotably drier air filters in. The bigger question mark is the \nsoutheast GA area ahead of the front. Outflow from the early \nmorning convection could effectively stabilize the environment \nand limit thunderstorm potential. But, if destabilization can \noccur, there could be a conditional severe threat as the \ncombination of peak heating and moisture pooling ahead of the \nfront could align across southeast GA in the afternoon hours. \nModel soundings indicate potential for ~30 kt of mid-level flow \nwith MLCAPE values into the 1,500-2,000 J/kg range. There are \neven indications of DCAPE values upwards of 900 J/kg. Such an \nenvironment should could yield an isolated damaging wind gust \nthreat for the region along and south of I-16. For now, this \nseems like the less likely solution and hi-res models runs are \nindeed favoring thunderstorm initiation south of the Altamaha. \nAny thunderstorm and severe threat should come to an end as the \nfront clears the area by the late afternoon hours. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Minor coastal flooding is possible along the \nCharleston and Colleton County coastline with the upcoming \nevening high tide. \n\nGusty northeast winds will develop along the southeast SC coast\ntoday behind a passing cold front. This northeast flow will \nproduce elevated tide levels, especially around the time of this\nevening's high tide (occurring around 1015 PM). The \nastronomical tide level is 5.5 ft MLLW, so it would require a \nrather anomalous 1.5 ft departure to reach the minor coastal \nflood threshold of 7 ft MLLW. For context, historical analysis \nshows that a departure of 1.5 ft or higher has only occurred \nwith ~7% of all minor coastal flood events on record. Still, \nminor coastal flooding is possible, and a Coastal Flood Advisory\ncould be needed for the Charleston and Colleton county coasts.",
